  "notes": "The scales came apart hardest on \"sharpened\", \"wiped\" and \"cleaned\": each is highly picturable and menacing in tone yet describes nothing that could injure anyone, so picturability sits far above violence and fatality is near floor. The reverse held for \"shoved\", \"slid\", \"stuck\", \"pushed\" and \"brought\", where the likely object is a body and fatality is substantial, but the sentence does not say what is entered or struck, holding picturability down. \"pointed\", \"aimed\" and \"waved\" are vivid and threatening with low fatality because the act itself does not wound; \"slit\" is the one word whose fatality exceeds its violence, since a slit throat is almost always lethal even when the described motion is small. I treated bare adverbs (casually, quickly, carefully, cautiously, gently, suddenly, slowly, then), bare pronouns and function words (I, the, with, what, a, she, he, it), the copula \"was\", and the underscore artifact as NOT_SCORABLE, since none of them names an action or event; eventive light verbs (did, began, started, proceeded, tried, decided) were scored with very low picturability instead."
